d time to recover  in the presence of nutrients when the  drug has been diluted to submic levels  we see that the majority of cells in the  population  fluoresce and they also filament it as  you can see in the image in the 432  minute mark  and so they fluorescent filamented  regardless of whether they go on to live  or to die  and so they they form these long  filaments because one of the gene that  is part of the SOS DNA damage response  is cell a and cell a inhibits cell  division until the cells have had  adequate time to repair damaged DNA  so we also see that in these images  there is one cell that is indicated by  the red arrow that at some point around  the 468 minute mark it splits into two  and then it further divides giving rise  to a micro Colony by the 540 minute mark  and it dilutes the fluorescence as it  divides so that is an example of a  persistor  so collectively these data suggests that  in persisters originating from slow or  non-growing E coli cultures that have  been treated with fluoroquinolones these  cells still suffer from DNA damage and  they depend on DNA damage repair in the  post-antibiotic treatment recovery  period to survive treatment  so we then asked what are the defenses  do these cells depend on to survive  treatment with these fluoroquinolones  so to address this we turn to genes that  encode these multi-drug efflux pumps  including the resistance nodulation the  division family of pumps including Acra  B and Tulsi and E coli  so this work was led by a former MD PhD  student Brandon Byrd together with a  former post-bac student blessing zenik  and we decided to look at the  contributions of these pumps because for  quinolones like levofloxacin and a new  fourth generation for quinlon called  delafloxacin there are substrates of  these multi-drug efflux pumps and so if  we're to delete genes and coding  components of these pumps like deleting  acrb or told C we see that the mics of  these drugs decreased considerably  compared 